\subsection{Assumptions for Problem 1}

To formulate the daily scheduling problem as a deterministic optimization model, the following operational assumptions are adopted.

  \begin{enumerate}

    \item \textbf{Complete satisfaction of electricity demand.}
    The residential load must be fully supplied in every scheduling interval. Load shedding, delayed consumption, and demand-side response are not permitted. Thus, the total electricity supplied by the external grid, photovoltaic system, and battery must be no less than the residential demand at all times.

    \item \textbf{Unidirectional interaction with the external grid.}
    The microgrid may purchase electricity from the external grid, but it may not sell electricity back to the grid or use the external grid as a storage facility. Grid-purchase power is therefore nonnegative. Since no grid-connection capacity is specified, the external grid is assumed to have sufficient capacity to supply any required purchase power.

    \item \textbf{Photovoltaic generation and curtailment.}
    Photovoltaic electricity is used only within the microgrid to supply the residential load or charge the battery. When photovoltaic generation exceeds the sum of the local demand and the available battery-charging capacity, the remaining photovoltaic electricity is discarded.

    \item \textbf{Mutually exclusive charging and discharging.}
    The battery cannot charge and discharge simultaneously. If \(P_t^{\mathrm{ch}}\) and \(P_t^{\mathrm{dis}}\) denote the charging and discharging powers, respectively, then
    \[
        P_t^{\mathrm{ch}}P_t^{\mathrm{dis}}=0,
        \qquad \forall t.
    \]

    \item \textbf{Cost components.}
    The operating cost in Problem 1 consists only of the cost of electricity purchased from the external grid. Photovoltaic generation is free, and battery degradation, maintenance, curtailment, and switching costs are neglected. 
  \end{enumerate}

\subsection{Operational Assumptions for Problem 2}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Information and commitment.} The purchase schedule is fixed at 00:00 each day. Current load and photovoltaic (PV) output are measurable for balancing within each 10-minute interval. Future realized values are unavailable to the controller.
  \item \textbf{Settlement.} Scheduled purchases are paid for in full. Emergency purchases cover the remaining load deficit at five times the normal tariff and cannot charge the battery. Unused energy has no refund or export revenue.
  \item \textbf{Storage and time resolution.} Storage efficiencies and operating limits remain constant; self-discharge and degradation are neglected. Power is represented by its interval-average value, with $\Delta t=1/6$ h. Stored energy is carried continuously between days.
\end{enumerate}

Storage parameters and initialization are specified below rather than treated as additional assumptions.

