\subsection{Initialization and evaluation period}
\label{sec:q2-initialization}

The simulation is initialized with 6000 kWh on January 1, 2025. The January 1 purchase row is zero; subsequent January rows use the nonnegative part of the preceding row's net-load curve, converted to energy. The unavailable final entry of that preceding row is replaced by the corresponding entry of row $d-2$, or by zero on January 2 when no earlier complete row exists. Storage is advanced with the feedback rule in Section~\ref{sec:q2-execution}. Under the time convention below, the first represented interval begins at 00:10 on January 1. Since the preceding ten-minute interval is not represented, the implementation carries the stated initial energy into the first modeled interval without an additional storage update. This is a boundary initialization convention, not a reconstructed observation. The saved warm-up yields 10800 kWh at the February 1 midnight decision.

The evaluation settles the 334 purchase rows dated February 1--December 31, 2025, matching \texttt{result2.xlsx}; January purchase-row costs are excluded. These rows cover February 1 at 00:10 through January 1, 2026 at 00:10. Thus the reported amount is an eleven-month purchase-row evaluation, not a twelve-month bill or an exactly midnight-to-midnight calendar-period total. Natural-day storage summaries use physical midnight boundaries instead. All six policies in the updated comparison share this initialization, evaluation period, and settlement rule; archived sensitivity experiments are identified separately in Section~\ref{sec:q2-results}.

