\section{Model 1: Solution to Question 1}


\subsection{Problem Reformulation}

Problem 1 is a deterministic day-ahead energy scheduling problem. 
The 24-hour horizon is divided into 144 consecutive 10-minute intervals. 
For each interval, the electricity price, residential load demand, and photovoltaic generation are assumed to be known in advance. The microgrid must determine the amount of electricity purchased from the external grid together with the charging and discharging schedule of the battery. Therefore, Problem 1 is transformed into a deterministic linear programming problem whose objective is to minimize the total daily electricity purchasing cost while satisfying the energy balance and the operational constraints of the battery.

\subsection{Data Preprocessing}

The original load demand and photovoltaic generation data are given in terms of power (kW), while the scheduling decisions in the optimization model are expressed in terms of energy (kWh). Since the scheduling horizon is divided into 10-minute intervals, we set \[ \Delta t=\frac{1}{6}\ \text{h}. \] Accordingly, the load demand and available photovoltaic energy in interval $t$ are converted as \[ L_t=P_t^{L}\Delta t, \qquad R_t=P_t^{PV}\Delta t. \]

The same conversion is applied to the battery charging and discharging limits. With a maximum charging/discharging power of $5000$ kW, the maximum transferable energy in one interval is $5000\times(1/6)=833.33$ kWh. After preprocessing, all energy-related quantities are expressed in kWh and aligned over the same 144 ten-minute scheduling intervals for subsequent optimization.

\subsection{Linear Programming Model}

Based on the processed data, we formulate Problem 1 as a deterministic linear programming model. The decision variables include grid electricity purchase, battery charging and discharging, curtailed photovoltaic energy, and battery energy level in each scheduling interval.

\subsubsection{Objective Function}

The objective is to minimize the total daily electricity purchasing cost. 
\[
\min J=\sum_{t=1}^{144} p_t g_t.
\]

\subsubsection{Energy Balance Constraint}

For each scheduling interval, the total electricity supply must equal the total electricity consumption. Therefore,

\[
g_t + R_t + d_t
=
L_t + c_t + w_t,
\qquad t=1,\ldots,144.
\]

The left-hand side represents the total electricity supply, including electricity purchased from the external grid $g_t$, available photovoltaic energy $R_t$, and battery discharge $d_t$. The right-hand side represents the total electricity use, consisting of residential demand $L_t$, battery charging $c_t$, and curtailed photovoltaic energy $w_t$. Since electricity selling to the external grid is not specified in the problem, any surplus photovoltaic energy that cannot be consumed by the load or stored in the battery is represented by $w_t$.


\subsubsection{Battery Dynamics}

Considering the charging and discharging efficiencies, the battery energy evolves according to

\[
s_{t+1}
=
s_t+\eta_c c_t-\frac{d_t}{\eta_d},
\qquad t=1,\ldots,144,
\]

where

\[
\eta_c=\eta_d=0.90.
\]

This equation describes the inter-temporal relationship between consecutive battery states.

\subsubsection{Battery Capacity and Power Constraints}

The battery energy must remain within its allowable operating range:

\[
1200 \le s_t \le 10800,
\qquad t=1,\ldots,145.
\]

Since the maximum charging and discharging power is $5000$ kW and each interval lasts $1/6$ h, the corresponding energy limit per interval is $833.33$ kWh. Hence,

\[
0\le c_t\le833.33,
\qquad
0\le d_t\le833.33,
\qquad t=1,\ldots,144.
\]

In addition,

\[
g_t\ge0,
\qquad
w_t\ge0,
\qquad t=1,\ldots,144.
\]

\subsubsection{Initial and Terminal Conditions}

The initial battery energy at 0:00 is given as

\[
s_1=6000\ \text{kWh}.
\]

To ensure a repeatable daily operating cycle, the battery energy at 24:00 is required to return to the same level:

\[
s_{145}=s_1=6000\ \text{kWh}.
\]

This terminal condition prevents the model from reducing the daily cost simply by exhausting the battery at the end of the day.

\subsubsection{Complete LP Formulation}

Combining the objective function and all operational constraints, the complete linear programming model for Problem 1 is summarized as follows:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\min \quad
& J=\sum_{t=1}^{144} p_t g_t \\[2mm]
\text{s.t.}\quad
& g_t+R_t+d_t=L_t+c_t+w_t,
&& t=1,\ldots,144,\\
& s_{t+1}=s_t+\eta_c c_t-\frac{d_t}{\eta_d},
&& t=1,\ldots,144,\\
& \eta_c=\eta_d=0.9,\\
& 1200\le s_t\le10800,
&& t=1,\ldots,145,\\
& 0\le c_t\le833.33,
&& t=1,\ldots,144,\\
& 0\le d_t\le833.33,
&& t=1,\ldots,144,\\
& g_t\ge0,\quad w_t\ge0,
&& t=1,\ldots,144,\\
& s_1=s_{145}=6000.
\end{aligned}
\]

All decision variables are continuous, and both the objective function and constraints are linear. Therefore, the resulting optimization problem is a standard linear programming problem.


